An event stream reconnects when the connection ends, fails or stays silent for the idle timeout: it asks again with fresh headers and `Last-Event-ID`, waits the server's `retry` value or `reconnect_delay` (1 second, doubling to 30 seconds with jitter), and raises the last failure after `max_reconnects` failures in a row without an event (5; 0 for a request with a body; `-1` is unlimited). It never reconnects after a 204, a status other than 408, 429 and 5xx, a response that is not `text/event-stream`, a cancellation or `client.close()`. An event named `error` raises `ApiError` with the response status and the data as its body, and a comment line such as a heartbeat counts as activity for the idle timeout. The generator option `sseTerminator` names a data value (such as `[DONE]`) that ends the stream and is not delivered. `StreamOptions` also takes `idle_timeout` and `last_event_id`.

### Pagination

An operation that returns a list one page at a time has a `<operation>_pages` method next to its plain method. It takes the same parameters in the same order and returns a `Paginator<T, C, B>`, where `T` is the item type, `C` is the type of the cursor parameter and `B` is the type of the page body. The method sends no request: the first request goes out when you first read an item or a page, and each next request goes out only when you ask for more, so stopping early sends nothing further and leaves nothing to close. Call `items()` for a `Stream` of the items, or call `pages()` for a `Stream` of `Page<T, C, B>` values with `items()`, `next_cursor()` and `result()`, the same `ApiResult` that the `_with_http_info` variant returns, so the status and headers stay reachable. Iterating again starts again from the starting position.
{{#hasPagedOperation}}

```rust file
use futures_util::StreamExt as _;

use {{packageName}}::{{clientClassName}};
{{#pagedOperation}}

async fn read_everything(
    client: &{{clientClassName}},
{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}
{{#isPath}}
    {{paramName}}: {{{dataType}}},
{{/isPath}}
{{#isBody}}
    {{paramName}}: {{#nullable}}Option<{{/nullable}}{{{dataType}}}{{#nullable}}>{{/nullable}},
{{/isBody}}
{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}
) -> Result<(), {{packageName}}::Error> {
    let list = client.{{#pagedGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/pagedGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.pagesMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}{{^isServer}}{{^isOptions}}{{paramName}}, {{/isOptions}}{{/isServer}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}{{#isOptions}}None{{/isOptions}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}});
    let mut items = list.items();
    while let Some(item) = items.next().await {
        println!("{:?}", item?);
    }
    let mut pages = list.pages();
    while let Some(page) = pages.next().await {
        let page = page?;
        println!("{} items, status {}", page.items().len(), page.result().status_code());
    }
    Ok(())
}
{{/pagedOperation}}
```
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the method is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is raised from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the iteration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `null` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent raises `PaginationError`, a direct child of `{{errorPrefix}}Error`, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: use `StreamExt::take`.

How each style ends and resumes: an id list passes the `last_id` or `next_cursor` of each page as the starting cursor of the next request and ends when a declared `has_more` is false. An offset list starts at the offset you pass, sends the offset past the items it received on each next request, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A page-number list sends the page you pass, or none, and then asks for the page after each one, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A next URL list takes the URL in the body of each page, follows it only within the origin of the client, and ends when the property is absent, `null` or empty. A cursor, offset or page that does not advance raises `PaginationError` as a token cursor does.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a method has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

A list whose page body carries the next URL in a property (a next URL list) is followed in the same way as a `Link` list: the URL is taken from the body of each response, a next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError`, nothing is sent to it, and such a method cannot resume from a saved position.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names and shape. The operation must be a GET with one JSON 2xx response, and it must match exactly one of these styles. A token cursor is one query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker`, paired with a next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type. An id cursor is one query parameter among `starting_after`, `after_id` and `after`, paired with a boolean `has_more` and a string `last_id` or `next_cursor` property. A `Link` list has a `Link` header declared on the response. An offset list has an `offset` query parameter with a `limit`, and a page-number list has a `page` query parameter with one of `per_page`, `page_size`, `size` or `limit`. A next URL list has no cursor parameter and a string property with `format: uri` or `uri-reference`, or one named `next_url`, `next_uri`, `nextlink` or `next_link`, that is optional or nullable. An operation that matches more than one style is ambiguous, and one that is streamed is never paged; either keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty, hasMoreProperty}`, where the style is `token`, `link`, `id`, `offset`, `page` or `body`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

The client's `with_http_client(authenticator, client, transport_options)` constructor sends
every request through a `reqwest::Client` you built yourself (a custom
connector, a shared pool, a test double). The SDK defaults (timeout,
`User-Agent`, request ids, default headers, redirect handling) still apply on
top. Build the client with `reqwest::redirect::Policy::none()` so the SDK's own
redirect handling stays in charge. You own the client's lifecycle: the SDK never
shuts it down. Setting a proxy, a CA certificate or `verify_ssl(false)` on
`TransportOptions` together with a supplied client fails with
`ConfigurationError::InvalidArgument`, because those belong to the client you
built.

### Testing your code

Every API group has an interface (`{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Trait{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`) and the client has one (`{{clientClassName}}Trait`), so a test can replace the SDK with a hand-written fake: no mocking library, no network.

```rust file
use {{packageName}}::api::{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Trait{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}};

// Code under test takes the group trait, never the generated struct.
fn use_group<A: {{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Trait{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}>(_api: &A) {}

// A fake implements the trait; the compiler lists the operations it still has to implement.
// use_group(&MyFake);
```

A new operation in the API adds a method to the trait, so a fake that implements the whole trait stops compiling after you regenerate; keep the fake in one place, or wrap the generated type and forward the operations you do not fake, so only the operation you fake is written.

### Credentials

`{{clientClassName}}::with_token(host, access_token, transport_options)` takes both
values as `Option<&str>`. The value you pass wins; when you pass `None` the
client reads the environment variable once, while it is built:
`{{envPrefix}}_BASE_URL` for the host and `{{envPrefix}}_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the token. An
empty variable counts as unset. A value that is neither passed nor set fails
the constructor with `ConfigurationError::InvalidArgument`, so a missing
credential never surfaces on the first request.

To renew a token without rebuilding the client, pass a callback instead:
`{{clientClassName}}::with_token_provider(host, provider, transport_options)` takes an
`Arc<dyn Fn() -> String + Send + Sync>` (`TokenProvider`) that is called for
every request, and `{{envPrefix}}_ACCESS_TOKEN` is not read.
{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

The authenticator written for each security scheme in the API does the same for
its own credentials. Every credential is an `Option<&str>` argument: the value
you pass wins, even an empty one (the underlying authenticator then rejects it);
when you pass `None` the variable below is read once, while the authenticator
is built, and an empty variable counts as unset. A credential that is neither
passed nor set fails `new` with `ConfigurationError::InvalidArgument` naming the
variable, so the failure comes before the client exists. A Bearer scheme's
authenticator also has `with_token_provider(host, provider)`, which takes the
token from a callback instead and reads no variable.

| Scheme | Field | Environment variable |
