### Per-call options

Every operation takes an `options` argument (`Option<&...Options>`), including an
operation with no parameters and no security. Besides the operation's own
optional parameters it carries three settings for that one call: `timeout(Duration)` replaces the client's end-to-end timeout (zero is
refused with `ConfigurationError::InvalidTimeout`), `max_retries(u32)` replaces
its retry budget (`0` turns retries off) and `header(name, value)` adds a header
that wins over the client's default headers and the operation's header
parameters. Authentication headers are applied after it.

### Hooks and telemetry

One optional hook runs around every operation call. It can edit the request headers before the send and observe the status or the error afterwards, and it does nothing when none is set. It runs once per call, however many sends happen inside it (retries, the one replay after a `401`), once for the plain and the `_with_http_info` variant alike, and also when you supply your own transport.

```rust file
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::time::Instant;
use {{packageName}}::{CallHook, CallInfo, CallObserver, CallOutcome, ConfigurationError};
use {{packageName}}::{{clientClassName}};

struct Timing;
struct Finish {
    operation_id: String,
    started: Instant,
}

impl CallHook for Timing {
    fn start(&self, info: &CallInfo, headers: &mut HashMap<String, String>) -> Option<Box<dyn CallObserver>> {
        headers.insert("X-Caller".to_string(), "billing".to_string());
        Some(Box::new(Finish { operation_id: info.operation_id.clone(), started: Instant::now() }))
    }
}

impl CallObserver for Finish {
    fn finish(self: Box<Self>, outcome: &CallOutcome<'_>) {
        println!("{} {} {:?}", self.operation_id, outcome.status_code, self.started.elapsed());
    }
}

fn timed_client() -> Result<{{clientClassName}}, ConfigurationError> {
    let client = {{clientClassName}}::with_token(Some("https://api.example.com"), Some("token"), None)?
        .with_call_hook(Arc::new(Timing));
    Ok(client)
}
```

`start(info, headers)` runs after the headers are final (defaults, operation headers, per-call headers, authentication, cookies and the propagated trace context), so it can read and replace any of them; reuse the spelling a header already has. A header it sets is on every send of the call. `info` carries `operation_id` (as written in the spec), `method`, `url_template`, `url` (without query string and fragment) and `server_address`. It returns the observer to call when the call ends, or `None`. `finish(outcome)` runs exactly once on every way the call can end, with `status_code` (`0` when no response arrived), the lower-cased response `headers` (`None` when no response arrived) and the `error` the caller receives (`None` on success); a call whose future is dropped finishes with `CallCancelled`. An error thrown by `start` (a panic) reaches the caller unchanged, nothing is sent and `finish` is not called; an error thrown by `finish` is swallowed. The hook never changes the result or the error of the call. Combine several hooks with `call_hook::chain(vec![first, second])`: they start in the order given and finish in the reverse order. Set a hook with `Client::with_call_hook` or `ConfigurationBuilder::call_hook`.

{{#hasOpenTelemetryAdapter}}
`OpenTelemetryCallHook` is the hook that records one span, one request count and one duration histogram per call with the OpenTelemetry API. Enable the `opentelemetry` feature (the SDK itself does not need the OpenTelemetry SDK), then pass `OpenTelemetryCallHook::new()` for the global providers or `OpenTelemetryCallHook::with_providers(&tracer_provider, &meter_provider)` for your own.

```rust file
use std::sync::Arc;
use {{packageName}}::{{clientClassName}};
use {{packageName}}::ConfigurationError;

fn traced_client() -> Result<{{clientClassName}}, ConfigurationError> {
    let client = {{clientClassName}}::with_token(Some("https://api.example.com"), Some("token"), None)?
        .with_call_hook(Arc::new({{packageName}}::OpenTelemetryCallHook::new()));
    Ok(client)
}
```

The span is named with the operation id, has kind client and carries `http.request.method`, `url.template`, `server.address`, `http.response.status_code` (only when a response arrived) and `error.type` (only on failure: the status code for a status of 400 or more, otherwise `timeout`, `network_error`, `serialization_error`, `cancelled` or `_OTHER`). The counter `http.client.request.count` (unit `{request}`) and the histogram `http.client.request.duration` (unit `s`) carry the same attributes; the histogram's own count equals the request count. The instrumentation scope is the published package name and the SDK version. The hook writes `traceparent` itself, so it names the client span as its parent. No attribute carries a query string, a header value or a credential.
{{/hasOpenTelemetryAdapter}}

### Errors

`ApiError` carries `request_method()` and `request_path()`, and so does the
`ApiErrorFields` of `ApiErrorKind`; the status wrappers (`NotFoundError`,
`ServerError`, `NetworkError`, ...) reach them through `api_error()`. The path
is the operation path without scheme, host, query string or fragment, so a
credential in a query string never reaches an error. The message names them:
`API returned status code 404 for GET /items/7`.

### Operations with several success responses

When an operation declares several 2xx responses with different schemas, it
returns the generated union (an enum with one variant per schema) and the body
is decoded as the variant the actual status code declares, so a `201` is never
read as the schema of the `200`. A 2xx status the operation does not declare is
decoded by the union itself.

### Models

A model is a plain value: bind it without `mut` and it cannot change. Every
property has a consuming `with_<property>(value)` method that returns the
model with that one field replaced, so `model.clone().with_name("x".to_string())`
is a changed copy and the original stays as it was. An optional property takes
the inner type and stores `Some(value)`.

### Behaviours to know

- **Optional versus nullable.** An optional property is an `Option<T>` whether or
  not its schema also allows `null`: an absent key and an explicit `null` both
  decode to `None`, and `None` is left out when the model is written. Only a
  required nullable property keeps the difference: its key must be present when
  decoding and is written as `null` when the value is `None`.
  A required nullable query parameter is kept the same way: when its value is `None` the key is still sent, with an empty value (`?key=`).
- **Unknown enum values decode to a catch-all.** A string that is not one of an enum's
  values decodes to its `UnknownDefaultOpenApi` variant, which has no wire value and
  cannot be written; a discriminator value that is not in the mapping still makes
  decoding fail with a `SerializationError` that names the value.
- **Repeated response headers.** `ApiResult::headers()` is a map with one entry
  per lower-cased name, and a header the server sent more than once is joined
  into that entry with `, ` in wire order (RFC 9110 section 5.3).
- **`Configuration` and `TransportOptions` are separate on purpose.**
  `Configuration` describes the API the operations talk to (the base URL and the
  headers an API group adds to its calls); `TransportOptions` configures the HTTP
  transport underneath (timeout, retries, proxy, TLS, redirects, request ids).
  The client builds the first from the authenticator and takes the second as an
  argument.

### Compression

Requests send `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br, zstd` unless you set that
header, and the response body is decoded from `gzip`, `deflate`, `br` or `zstd`
before it reaches you; `Content-Encoding: identity` passes through.

### Logging and tracing

The crate does not log. With the `opentelemetry` feature each request carries the
W3C `traceparent` of the span that is current when you call the operation; to hand a span to a call, wrap the call in `.with_context(cx)` (`opentelemetry::trace::FutureExt`). Without the feature, inject your own through
`TransportOptions` default headers, or wrap the calls in your own spans.

